Ralph Schmidt <laire@t-online.de> wrote:
>In article <9hig3h$p18$1@pegasus.csx.cam.ac.uk>, "Nick Maclaren"
><nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk> wrote:
>
>> 
>> The problem is that some interrupts apply to a well-defined thread
>> (and hence are no trouble) and others are more global.  Consider
>> clock or I/O interrupts, for example.  Or consider the following
>> scenario:
>> 
>>     Thread A is handling a TLB miss or in-memory page fault, and
>> will be uninterruptible for the best part of a microsecond.
>> 
>>     A high-priority fault comes in (say clock, I/O or machine check),
>> so thread B takes it.
>> 
>>     The kernel code then needs to lock all threads while it handles
>> the interrupt.
>
>I can't see the significant difference to SMP either you try to
>explain here.

That is exactly my point!  If you want to allow multiple CPU threads
to run in different modes, you end up with going back to the original
SMP designs, where they shares all cache levels but were otherwise
separate.  I am not saying that this is a bad idea, but it ISN'T
anything radically different from SMP.

If you want the CPU to be able to multi-thread serial code (as is
needed to run most existing codes fast), then you need a lower level
of threading, and it is virtually inevitable that you will have to
require all threads to run in the same mode.  And this is what I
think is being investigated in some chips due in 2002 and 2003.


