>
>Were the low-end RS/6000s available in 1989 
>(a) not single chip or
>(b) not super scalar?
>
>It was my understanding that even the very first RS/6000s were
>superscalar, and I would have thought the low-end ones were single CPU.

The original RS/6000 had about five chips: FPU, ALU, instruction unit,
and data caches.  (The multiplier alone was comparable in size to the
ROMPC chip in the IBM RT, according to the architecture book IBM
published.) There was also a lot of control logic on the processor
board to handle startup and diagnostics.

The first single-chip RS/6000 was the 220/230 using the "RSC" chip,
which evolved into the 601 with a lot of help from Motorola.


