Paul Brown <pol@read.the.sig.coz.this.address.is.invalid> writes:

>Then you have the i860 which was a compiler nightmare

Alliant's compiler group did pretty well with it, but since they were
in a Fortran-centric arena, they got real good writing fast assembler
routines for the compiler to call.

Alliant's OS group (and Stratus's) managed to write exception handlers
that _worked_, something Intel never achieved.  At that time if you
worked at Intel and didn't work on x86 you were dirt.  If you worked
on software you were dirt.  If you worked on non-x86 non-hardware you
were below dirt.  Intel's SysV port to i860 never made it to market
and several vendors attempting i860 workstations were left with piles
of broken stuff.

I trust working on IA-64 doesn't have the same stigma!

