Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam (Larry Kilgallen) writes:
> Still going strong, but based on marketing, market share and good system
> design.  _Not_ based on leading edge CPU performance.

I disagree.  IBM has many customers for which the descendents of the 360
offer the best CPU performance for their applications.  The CPU
performance of the latest chips may not quite be on par with a 1.4G
Athlon, but they're still quite respectable.  They're still driven by
performance as a major design objective.  In other words, if something
that made the CPU chip cost 3x as much would deliver 30% better
performance, they'd seriously consider it, because the CPU chip cost is
an insignificant part of the system.

By way of comparison, the 68000 family, which I cited as an example of
processors that are still made and in common use, are definitely no longer
trying to compete on performance.  Now their focus is cost.

[Geez, if you had told me ten years ago that I'd one day be defending
IBM mainframes (which I've hardly ever even used), I'd have said you
were insane.]


