In article <oupsngj351a.fsf@pigdrop.muc.suse.de>,
Andi Kleen  <freitag@alancoxonachip.com> wrote:
>david20@alpha1.mdx.ac.uk (D.Webb) writes:
>> 
>> SMT on EV8 would not have provided any significant performance improvements ???
>
>Not for a single threaded application.
>
>It is still very hard to multithread software and a lot of the existing
>code isn't. And the scaling curve of such ports often does not look very
>good. Also see Amdahl's law.
> 
>-Andi

SMT benefits *independent* threads as well as multi-threaded applications.
The latter may be what benchmarks measure, but the former has much broader
applicability.  Full exploitation of the benefits may require an SMT-aware
OS (to schedule threads that are mutually cache-friendly, assuming we can
figure out how to measure that, for example).  After all, SMT is similar
to SMP, except that some processors are closer to each other than others.
Also, for instructions at least, sharing among independent threads is not
negligible (think shared libraries).

Michel


