andrew@gurney.reilly.home (Andrew Reilly) writes:
> The Alpha was (very publicly) architected to support a 25 year
> working life.  That was the point of not having frilly bits like
> branch delay slots.
[...]
> Not to mention all of the archaic 8-bit embedded processors,
> 8051, PIC, 6800 derivatives that are much, much older and still
> very much in active use and development.

The Alpha was intended to have a 25-year life as a leading-edge
processor for workstations, servers, etc.  The 25-year life wasn't
intended to include declining days in low-end embedded applications,
where the 8 and 16 bit processors you're comparing to are now found.

For example, the 68000 family is still very much around, but its life
as a general-purpose computing architecture only lasted from 1979 to
about 1994.  Still a quite respectable run.

Compare to the IBM 360 architecture, though, which is still going strong
for high-end computing at 37 years.  (AFAIAC, you can call the newer
stuff 370, ESA, z-series, or whatever, but there's still most of a 360
architecture inside.)

Maybe the x86 architecture will last as long as the 360, if Intel
doesn't succeed at replacing it with IA64.


