In article <ESDZ3Kv7OCSG@cc.usu.edu>, Roger Ivie <ivie@cc.usu.edu> wrote:
>In article <3B3B4580.25C255DD@zk3.dec.com>, Tony Verhulst <verhulst@zk3.dec.com> writes:
>> Look at the history of CPU architectures VAX, MC680x0, and others.  They
>> tend not to last more than 10 to 12 years.
>
>You mean others like:
>
>  - VAX (11/780 -> NV5 sure _seemed_ like more than 12 years)

VAX was competitive by most standards for 5-8 years.  RISC made
it uncompetitive for performance, but VAX was still attractive for
people locked into VMS or for environments where the rest of the
system was more important than the CPU.   NVAX was an impressive
accomplishment but they were only made for people who weren't
ready to move to Alpha.  They weren't better than the competition,
just more backwards-compatible.

>  - MC680x0 (Macintosh -> Palm sure seemed like more than 12 years)

As a general purpose CPU, 68000 series lasted about 12 years.
It was dead with the arrival of PowerPC; the 68040 was the
last successful version.  Embedded 68K series chips did much
better.  Years after the 68060 failed to attract interest in
the general purpose market people were still building hardware
control systems using descendants of the 68000.   (And most
of these chips are not based on the 68030 or 68040.)


>  - PDP-8 (PDP-5 in 1963 -> DECmate III until about 1993)
>  - PDP-11 (Call it 1970, still being built)

The last new DEC PDP-11 chip was c. 1984.  (Well, technically I think
it was the first and last PDP-11 on a chip.)  The old design was
manufactured for many more years.  Does "still being built" refer to
a new chip?

>  - 8080 (Early '70s, lives on in the Z80, still being extended)
>  - 8051 (I don't know when it started, but it's been longer than 12 years)
>  - 8048, for that matter (runs the keyboard I'm typing on)
>  - x86, of course (much, much too long)

That's an exception, but you should start the clock with the 80386.

>  - 360 -> 370 -> 390
>  - Burroughs 5500 -> Unisys A-series
>
>In fact, the vast majority of machines I use run architectures which are
>much older than 12 years and are still in active development.

Most of the ones you mentioned survive as embedded processors only.
The architecture mostly stopped evolving.  The 68K series went backwards,
implementing the 68000 or 68020 ISA for many embedded chips instead of
the 68030+. 

