John Bayko <jbayko@sk.sympatico.ca> writes:
>     The Intel i960, at one point the world's most popular 32-bit
> embedded CPU, was tossed without regard when Intel switched to ARM. ARM
> may have been a better architecture, but the i960 was not at all bad,
> and had much life left in it - even superscalar versions, which ARM
> doesn't have.

Of course, the i960 was originally intended to be a CPU for servers
and workstations.  It had a 33-bit tagged architecture, microcoded
object operations (the legacy of the iAPX 432), and system software
written in Ada.  The CPU was code-named P7, and the project was "Gemini".
It was developed and for a short time marketed by the Intel/Siemens
joint venture BiiN.

Only when that failed did they get rid of the tag bit and microcode,
call it i960, and push it for embedded applications.  A very smart
move in hindsight.

The tag bit and microcode came back for the i960-M series, which they
tried to sell for military systems.  They hyped the object-oriented
and software-reliability features.  I'm not sure how many M-series
parts were sold, but I'd wager that very few of them were really used
in systems that took advantage of the tag bit and microcode.

Of course, the ARM was originally intended to be a CPU for servers and
workstations also.  History repeats itself.




