>  - Burroughs 5500 -> Unisys A-series
>
>In fact, the vast majority of machines I use run architectures which
>are much older than 12 years and are still in active development. 

Bad examples.

You are mixing up CPU architecture and implementation.  The development
of the 680x0 CPU *architecture* stopped many years ago.  They still
*implement* 68000's in newer processes, and with higher system
integration; but the actual ISA in use has actually *regressed* (most
new 680x0 chip implementations use the 68000 ISA, NOT the 68020/30/40
ISA).  Palm not only is not pushing the architecture of the main CPU
they use, they're jumping ship to ARM.

A case can be made that the Z80 is still alive architecturally, since
people still try to enhance the Z80 ISA (architecture) by adding
instructions and CPU functional units, and not by just moving the old
design to new CAD tools, integrating LCD controllers, etc. and doing die
shrinks.


IMHO. YMMV.
