Andi Kleen wrote:
> 
> Martin H�yer Kristiansen <martin@netimage.dk> writes:
> >
> > Interestingly, what killed the 68K architecture was the serial decoding
> > of some instructions, which made superscalar 68K MPUs (68060) a
> > nightmare to design, the 68060 was late and underperformed.
> 
> Just curious: which instructions were that?

Most instructions. The problem where the different types of adressing,
in particular the memory-indirect modes. The original 68K instructions
consisted of a instruction word followed by an extensio word or
double-word; ie. 2, 4 or 6 bytes in length. The 68020 introduced a whole
bunch of extra adressing modes, which was implemented by making
extensions to the extension word(s). They BIG problem was that the total
length of the instruction was _not_ immidiately available, and thus
hampering superscaler decode.

Motorola went on to introduce Coldfire, which disposed of all the 68020
crud (and then some).

Cheers
Martin


