In article <1br8w41re8.fsf@cs.nmsu.edu>,
 Joe Pfeiffer <pfeiffer@cs.nmsu.edu> writes:
>In the first Alpha paper I read, in the DEC Technical Journal, Alpha's
>lifespan was estimated at 25 years (based in part on the history of
>the VAX, which went nearly that long), and that DEC anticipated a
>1000-fold performance improvement over that lifespan.

That stuff was probably written by some marketroid, because that
factor of 1000 was to be achieved in the following way:

1) factor 10 by higher clock rate
2) factor 10 by more ILP
3) factor 10 by multiprocessing

Ad 1) If we assume that Moore's law continues to hold during the 25
years, much more than a factor of 10 should be possible by clock rate
(e.g., IA-32 has seen a factor of 25 since Alpha was introduced,
although Alpha has only seen a factor of 4, for whatever reasons).  I
would expect around a factor of 1000 over 25 years.

Ad 2) A factor of 10 more ILP than the 21064 is extremely unlikely,
except on vectorizable code (and it looked unlikely to me when Alpha
was introduced).

Ad 3) With multiprocessing you can get as much as you can afford
(e.g., ASCI Q), but only on parallelizable applications (or lots of
independent tasks).

So it seems that this factor of 1000 and its components were not based
on any technical considerations, but because it looked impressive, and
the components were chosen such that they did not look too outrageous.

>  So I'm curious
>as to what went wrong -- what didn't pan out?

Alpha did not sell enough.

Followups to comp.arch.

