In article <993820703.988205@haldjas.folklore.ee>,
Sander Vesik <sander@haldjas.folklore.ee> writes:
|> In comp.arch Martin Knoblauch <Martin.Knoblauch@teraport.de> wrote:
|> >  do threads have to come from one single application to make use of SMT
|> > (at least as it was planned for EV8)?
|> 
|> No, not at all. And unless they screwed something up, one thread would be
|> able to be ay doing TLB reload for itself while some other thread was
|> doing computation, etc. 

This has been tried before - it didn't work then, either.

Most systems have modes (kernel and other, if nothing else).  Now,
consider the problems of allowing the two threads to be associated
with applications running in different modes.  There are basically
two solutions:

    1) Replicate enough of the state so that you are really
talking about SMP-on-a-chip and not SMT any longer.

    2) Insist that all CPU threads are associated with the same
application, and probably same process.

You might be able to introduce a kludge by which TLB reload and
in-memory page faults were included in this, but great care would
be needed.  Beyond that, madness lies.


