In comp.arch, google@wot-club.org.uk (Chris Rijk) wrote:

>"del cecchi" <dcecchi@msn.com> wrote in message news:<BZa_6.158$wt2.7991@eagle.america.net>...
>> Check out Northstar/Pulsar/ISTAR/Sstar series.
>
>AFAIK That chip has "vertical threading" (or coarse grained threading
>or whatever you want to call it). If I remember correctly it holds
>two thread states on the core, and swaps between them on L1 cache
>misses (and other events I guess) with a 3 cycle swap penalty to move
>between threads. I think it has a 5 stage pipeline...
>
>This isn't SMT, where multiple threads can be in all/most stages
>of the pipeline at the same time. (though I'm sure people will
>be picky with my description). VT can be very useful but not as
>good as SMT I think.

Another way of putting it: with SMT several instructions from
different threads can be executing at the same stage of execution in
different functional units at the same time (ie in the same cycle).

It took me a while to get the difference between SMT and more
coarse-grain threading. There is a very nice figure illustrating it
at:

http://www.realworldtech.com/page.cfm?ArticleID=RWT122600000000



