> Peter Boyle wrote:
> [...]
> > Intel didn't have to chop a flagship to start StrongArm sales.
>
>     The Intel i960, at one point the world's most popular 32-bit
> embedded CPU, was tossed without regard when Intel switched to ARM. ARM
> may have been a better architecture, but the i960 was not at all bad,
> and had much life left in it - even superscalar versions, which ARM
> doesn't have.



Yes, but one might say, who needs slow but superscaler when you can have an
embedded core running at 800 MHz?  When Intel made the decision, i960 was
running at something like 100 MHz and StrongArm, without any of Intel's
process stuff was running at 200 MHz.  I have heard rumors that SA3
(whatever it will be called) will be superscaler.  SA-2, aka Xscale, can
overlap loads with the following compute instructions, as ling as they don't
need the loaded value.  Plus StrongArm had much better power
characteristics, which are important for many of the growth markets that
Intel wants to get into.  I don't think you would ever see an i960 in a cell
phone or a PDA.  IMO, Intel made the right choice, even though it cost them
something in customer loyalty, etc.


