>   Message to Alpha designers:  Which chip can your RISC experience help the
>   most?  The answer is not Itanium but Power or UltraSPARC.  Now, which company
>   deserves your loyalty and would appreciate your contributions more?  Intel
>   or IBM and Sun?

A number of Alpha designers - the ones that left after the last merger -
went to AMD. It's not a RISC chip, but the internal architecture of the
Athlon (after the x86 decode step) looks quite similar to the Alpha; a
bit less agressive, though. After all, if there is no chance but design
Intel-like chips, would you design for or against Intel? Furthermore,
AMD now wants compiler experts (for Hammer), too.

Perhaps Intel really wants wetware, but what it will get is just the
architecture. A good backup if IA64 fails. After all, much of Intel's
success are "backup" solutions. Ditch i432, ship 8086. Ditch i860, ship
Pentium. Intel's history is a good example of the "worse is better"
theorem, because they always invested a lot of money in overengineered
architectures that caused one or the other problem (i860 mostly was a
compiler and OS nightmare, not so much overcomplex hardware as i432 and
Itanic; Itanic is perhaps the worst of both: overcomplex hardware and
compiler/OS nightmare), and the cash cow was some more evolutionary
developed design.

