In comp.sys.dec Sergei Morozov <smv@stanford.edu> wrote:
: Eric Bennett wrote:

:>
:> We buy our Alphas for the performance of the chip, not for the OS, so once
:> Compaq has thrown away its advantage over the competition by selling the
:> same CPUs everyone else is selling... why should they still get our
:> business?

: Furthermore, it'll take years to port Tru64, and even then it'll be miles behind Linux 
: (or even, god forbid windows), which is what everyone will be
: selling with IA-64. Tru64 is great as long as it's the best OS for the best platform.

While I'm not at all pleased at the announcement, I think that the above is not
a well-founded statement...

1) Tru64 *has* been ported to IA-64 for the most part already - remember
"Bravo", the port they were working on w/ Sequent and someone else (can't
remember who?)...This port supposedly was largely completed before Sequent
got bought by IBM and bailed on the effort...My guess is that to wrap up the
port and finish testing, compatibility, etc. will take at best 9 months or
so.  I wouldn't be suprised to see Tru64 on IA-64 available in some limited
form yet this year or early next...

2) It would take a *long* time for Tru64 to fall behind Linux, I'm
sorry...Linux is all fine and well, and a nice OS for certain tasks, but
it's nowhere near as robust, scalable, or complete as Tru64.  Where Linux
has an advantage is in ISV support at the moment, although I'm doubtful
we'll see Oracle clusters on Linux anytime real soon, frex.

3) Look at the potential competition on IA-64:

     AIX 5L+ (formerly known as Project Monterrey)
     Linux
     Some sort of BSD
     Windows 2000/XP

   The only real Unix competition on the platform will be Linux/BSD, as I
   doubt that people will go diving for AIX on IA-64.  Windows is a
   competitor today, so I don't see how that's changed much.  If (and it is
   a big if) Intel/Compaq can deliver a truly usable Itanium, than Tru64 on
   IA-64 (& OpenVMS on IA-64, for that matter) has simply leveled the 
   hardware playing field.  When I work with customers in the field, the big
   thing that pushed everybody to Windows in the first place was hardware
   cost more than anything.  A *lot* of them weren't happy with the move
   either.  This move on Compaq's part could potentially give people like
   me more of chance to push Unix over Windows then I have today.  Let's
   face it, while Linux & *BSD are making in-roads, it's slow and corporate
   adoption is *not* what some would want you to believe.  A solid Unix 
   available at a competitive price on "commodity" hardware would be a 
   very easy sell in a *lot* of the customer sites I've worked.  

In short, while dumping the Alpha is a questionable strategy, I can't see
how this can hurt Tru64's chances any more than they were last week.  Last
week you had to sell people on a complete architecture switch - in the
future, you will hopefully be able to sell them on an OS switch (and with
the rate and pace of Microsoft & OS switches in recent years, that's an
easier battle to fight, IMHO...That's why Linux *has* been as successful as
it has.)

Now that I've left myself open as flamebait, I'm going to sit back and see
what develops over the next couple of years.  I'm somewhat more comfortable
with the future of Tru64 and VMS than I was before this announcement. 
Intel's got a lot of money to drop into building and rebuilding IA-64 until
they get it right, and from the numbers that people are starting to talk
about, it was only a matter of time before they would be neck and neck with
Alpha (okay, maybe a few years, but look at the glacial pace Alpha's always
moved at anyways...).  Given a neck and neck fight, I doubt Compaq would
have been able to keep up with a juggernaut like Intel - look how much
effort AMD expends doing it today.

Now that I think about it, I wonder what/how our reactions would have been
if Compaq had announce the sale of Alpha to AMD instead of Intel?  I'm
wondering how much my own anti-Wintel bias may be clouding or influencing my
opinions on the matter...

Chris

