<http://www.zonaresearch.com/deliverables/qemail/FNemail062501.htm>

The easy way to understand it is purely politically. Historically, Intel
make poor
processors. One has to remember that RISCs were introduced 15 years ago
and 64-bit CPUs about 10 years ago. Intel still do not make a mainstream
RISC
processor* nor a viable 64-bit processor. The only way Intel can
properly compete
in the high-end is by pre-emptively removing the competition, which is
precisely
what they have done. All the high-end architecture manufacturers (apart
from
Sun and DEC) were scared into oblivion by the Intel IA-64 bit
announcements
made since 1995 (!!), MIPS / SGI simply gave up. HP were some of the
main
contributors to the IA-64 architecture - they had had one of the fastest
architectures,
but essentially HP handed over their expertese to a competitor in return
for dropping
their own product (hmmm, a one-way deal!). DEC suddenly found itself
starved of engineers and was therefore architecturally finding it
harder. Then
there was the 'lawsuit', which can be fairly stated as:

    DEC: "Intel, you've stolen our patents!'
    Intel: "OK, we'll take your company too!"

A bizarre turn of events, matched only by Compaq's acquisition of
the Alpha, when priorly Compaq was built on the foundation of x86. And,
looking at Alpha's subsequent development looks to me like it was
actually being
stalled until it was no longer economically viable.

In that context, Compaq's announcement makes perfect sense and all that
remains
in terms of competition is Sun and PowerPC in the form of BookE (which
oddly enough
Motorola seem rather ambivalent about).

Conclusion: Being annoyed doesn't help, being aware does.

-cheers from jules @P.

*StrongArm notwithstanding, which of course, Intel got with DEC. You
might also
think the i960 was a RISC device, but I would beg to differ on account
of its
complex microcoded architecture :-)



