Cat is out of the bag. The 180 day memo is at:

http://www.theinquirer.net/23060107.htm

Note: on my browser, the text did not appear, I had to look at the html source
and format it to be readable.

Some things to remember here: Compaq is about to embark on multiple platforms,
something which it never had to do. It will now have to build and support both
8086 and IA64 in its mainstream products. (remember that Alpha stuff was
always relegated to its small independant corner and Compaq made sure ALpha
didn't disturb any of Compaq's real products). So the stuff about
standardizing on production techniques, reducing part numbers etc etc may be
targetted more at the 8086-IA64 platforms. 


Furthermore, VMS gets only a token mention in the text (we'll continue to
support our VMS customers" type of message). VMS doesn't even get its own
paragraph. After the "we'll continue to support VMS" the text switches to
Compaq's Linux strategy.

Since Capellas goes through great lengths to mention how True64 is growing,
rumours that Compaq would abandon Tru64 in favour of Linux are probably exagerated.

The big announcement in that memo is the fact that Compaq wants to transform
itself into a services and solutions company. It wanst its services to grow
40% per year from its current 1/5 of Compaq to the target of one third of
Compaq. They will be spending $500 million to acquire companies to grow their
services and solutions business.

The other important chaneg I read is that Compaq will start to deal with
customers and sell to them more then just the hardware. This means that Compaq
wants to do as Digital used to do before Palmer cut the sales force: sell
direct to enterprise customers and sell much mroe than just the hardware.
Whether this means that Compaq will start buying software (eg: IBM buying
Lotus) is another question.

Remember that in its heydays, Digital had a pretty impressive portfolio of
software products you could buy from Digital and get support from Digital. 


As far as Alpha is concerned:

There is no direct mention in the memo. Perhaps Charlie Matco knows more.
However, consider the costs to migrate VMS and tru64 to IA64. Compaq just paid
a lot of money to modify Alpha to support NSK. How much would Compaq have to
pay to get the IA64 modified to support VMS and NSK, or how much would VMS and
NSK engineers have to spend to change their OS to run on IA64 ?

Compare that to the costs of productising NT on Alpha (remember that NT
already runs on Alpha, it is just not productised). Consider that today, you
need far less software on NT-Alpha to make it attractive: SQL server and IIS
web server. And get Office to run on Alpha for desktops.

If Compaq asks the few remaining VMS ISVs to start to support VMS, Alpha and
IA64, that may be just too much to ask. Remember that during the VAX to Alpha
transition a whole bunch of products didn't make the transition.

Now, if Compaq were ready to make a BIG push for VMS to open it to any/all
markets, then perhaps the move to more commodity hardware would make sense
since you'd gain more customers than you'd lose. But in its current state, and
with Compaq clearly not interested in growing VMS more than it has to, I doubt
that migrating to IA64 would be a good thing.

Compaq has just succeeded is gaining enough confidence from VMS customers to
start spending on VMS a bit. If Compaq now turns around and ditches Alpha,
that confidence will go away VERY FAST.

As I understand it, Alpha is currently structured such that Compaq only has
the engineering of the chip left, and its manufacture is done by outside firms
9Samsung, IBM and does Intel still make them?)

I could see a drive to format the Alpha chip to fit into motherboards that are
also used for IA64. That would allow Compaq to share motherboard designs
between IA64 and Alpha servers and use much the same hardware for the two
platforms. But to do that, you do not need to sell Alpha to Intel. As a matter
of fact, you do not need to sell Alpha.

I would hope that if Compaq is to sell Alpha, it will sell it to an outfit
that wants to make Alpha grow, not sell it to the Borg whose sole purpose is
to assimilate the engineers and the technology.


