To me, a pivotal paragraph was:

"I see this as a great opportunity for Compaq -- if we step up to it. We
know how to deliver the industry standard building blocks. We also have the
ability to create substantial customer value above the building blocks
through innovation, services, partnerships, mission critical skills and
expertise in some key horizontal and industry-specific solutions."

When combined with the preceding statements:

"the current downturn ... has accelerated the use of industry standard
building blocks to develop low-cost IT solutions. Commoditization has moved
well beyond personal computers to the lower tiers of the infrastructure,
including servers and low-end storage."

and the later:

"We need to simplify for the customer. By that I mean simplify the IT
architecture to make it easier for customers to deploy. Simplify our
products and services -- including fewer SKUs, fewer part numbers and easier
access to service and support. Simplify our customer value proposition. And
simplify the buying experience -- however customers choose to buy from us.

We need to drive greater simplification in our organization. The simpler we
are -- and the better aligned we are upstream and downstream -- the more
effective we'll be in our execution. A simpler organization will also make
it easier for us to do everything once, and only once. Today, we tend to do
many things several times -- resulting in increased cost and slower
execution.

We also need to simplify our messages and communications. We've made some
good progress on this score, but we can do even better."

and even later:

"With our strong positions in access, industry standard servers and storage,
we have the building blocks, but we need to take immediate action to improve
our competitive position."

the belief that 'industry-standard' solutions - and *only* industry-standard
solutions (see the "once, and only once" reference above) - is the real
future seems unequivocably clear, though selling the existing high-end
systems will obviously continue as long as there are a reasonable number of
buyers out there.

Consider the emphasis on acquiring enterprise services companies rather than
attempting to capitalize on the organizations acquired from DEC and Tandem
as another indication that those existing organizations aren't relevant to
the vision of the future.  And:

"We are also consolidating our strategy and technology organizations. This
will insure that technology development and corporate strategic planning are
more tightly aligned."

may be subject to interpretation but does not inspire confidence in renewed
high-end intiatives (or even continuation at their current level:  if
Terry's right, there's a good chance that virtually *all* future VMS
expenditures will be devoted to migrating it to IA64, which may be
convenient for Compaq if they sell off Alpha but won't do a damn bit of good
for VMS customers if the system is otherwise in maintenance mode).

Capellas seems to have a major problem with 'the vision thing'.  Why Compaq
should be given a ghost of a chance when matched up against the likes of
IBM, SUN, and Dell is beyond my comprehension.

- bill





