Darryl,

To go much further in figuring out what sort of Pentium processors you
would be able to run in the DECpc XL 466d2, I would need to know the
DEC part number (either 50-series or 54-series) inscribed on the main
logic board.

If you look closely at the socket on the processor card, it is labeled
either Socket 2 or (most likely) Socket 3, and it is either blue or
white, with a row of empty socket holes around the 486 processor
currently installed. 

The Pentium OverDrive processor intended for Socket 2/3 fills all the
socket holes.  It was designed by Intel to give some 486s a limited
Pentium upgrade.  Maximum speed is 83MHz, and it is a true Pentium
processor with the smaller data bus used by 486 processors.

... Ben Myers

