Michael Dell.

According to your leaks, it seems that Alpha is dead, and the technologies
allowing it to do stuff such as Galaxies will be transfered over to IA64. In
essence, Compaq is capitulating to Intel and will have even less intelectual
property (value). VMS will be running on wintel boxes.

Considering that the migration from VAX to Alpha isn't complete, I am not sure
that the migration to IA64 will be so welcome and I am not sure that customers
will trust Compaq, especially those who invested mega money in very expensive
wildfire systems. They will see Compaq as abandonning VMS customers and
relegating VMS to some commodity low quality hardware (intel).

Remember that the remaining customer base is still on VMS for a reason. And if
they are going to be forced to migrate, my guess is that they will go to Sun
or IBM, vendors they can trust. Hopefully Compaq can attract enough new
low-quality customers (low end) at the same pace as the bigger customers
migrate away.

One thing this will help though is internal infighting. If VMS will run on the
same hardware as NT, then it may generate more synergy inside of the various
warring camps at Compaq.

If Compaq intends to change CEO, it should do so before the abandonnement of
Alpha is announced publicly.

Compaq is going to have to thread very carefully with its spinning of this
change because it could easily be interpreted as another step towards
abandonning the Digital products.

I would personally prefer to see Compaq get new manufacturers to start making
Alpha boxes and push Alpha. But I doubt this will happen. In the end, Alpha
will have lived about 15 years, far less than the 8086.


