> but the brightness is only about 1/2 what it should be.  This is with
> the bright/contrast all the way up.  Inside, if you turn up the screen
> voltage you get a raster, but it is still about 1/2 brightness.
> 
> Anyone have any ideas on what to check in this beast?

	Get yourself a multimeter and a high-voltage probe, and check the HV 
on the second anode lead. I seem to recall it should be around 12 KV or so. 
DO NOT ATTEMPT THIS ALONE if you've never done it before! Find someone 
experienced with testing procedures for high voltage circuits.

	Next thing would be to get hold of a CRT tester and run the tube 
through its paces. I figure either your HV section is going weak or the 
electron gun in the tube itself isn't putting out all the oomph that it 
should be.

	Two other possibilities: The brightness pot itself is shorting out, 
or the CRT has developed a shorted grid (the tester will find out if the 
latter is the case).

	If ANY of the above sounds unfamiliar to you, then you should most 
definitely enlist the aid of an experienced EE student, instructor, or 
other hardware techie type before poking around inside an active monitor. 
There's enough in the way of high voltage and other nastiness in there to 
be a significant risk to life and limb if you don't know what you're doing.

