  Sure, you can use "standard" -- and you really hit the mark with
  with your "whatever that means" comment -- hardware.  Depending on 
  the Alpha system, yes, you can use commodity parts, with the caveat 
  that some of the commodity stuff does not meet its own specification.
  Most Alpha systems tend to push the memory and the disk hard, too.
  Various Alpha systems will accept commodity hardware, and some require 
  specialized...

I mean, if I buy a diskless alpha, or a mobo + CPU combo... 
:will I be able to hook in any SCSI disk? What level of SCSI disk? Wide? 
:Ultra? 

  I'd want a SCSI controller that is supported by OpenVMS, and the 
  disks that match the particular SCSI controller...

:What about graphics? 

  A subset of graphics controllers are supported -- I'd probably look
  to a PowerStorm 3D30 or 4D20 (used) or an ELSA GLoria Synergy, 
  PowerStorm 300 or PowerStorm 350, or a 3DLabs Oxygen VX1 (new).
  There are other and older graphics controllers around.  

:What about monitors (if the box had an  original graphics card).

  Please read through the relevent portion of the OpenVMS FAQ.

  Read up on the synch-on-green stuff in the OpenVMS FAQ.

:- I've seen some "clonic" alpha boxes, usually running linux. Can I load 
