> first and forth, second and third.  So if I had
> 
> af46 00c9
> 
> I want
> 
> c900 46af
> 
> as output.

OK, here is a perl 1 liner which acts as a filter to do this:

perl -e 'while((sysread STDIN, $buffer, 4)>0) { syswrite(STDOUT,
reverse($buffer), 4, 0);}'

(this may not handle the case where the input file is not 4*something in
size correctly, and as
per the perl documentation, one should really handle interrupted system
reads and partial writes, see
the perl documentation of syswrite for details.
> 
> Also, I coudn't figure out how to pipe the dd output.  I tried od=\| and
> it created a file called '|'.

You don't need to specify of=| to dd to make it act as a filter, just
leave out
the of= specification; if dd had done the job then,
dd conv=swab <infile | od
would have displayed stuff.
