Folks,

Here's a writeup I did on the last round of issue submission and voting at
www.compaqworkinggroup.org. It's from a recent issue of SKC, so it reflects
a summary analysis of the Top Ten issues, not Compaq's feedback. (All 67
browser-choking slides of it!)

So even if you can't access the site, or have JavaScript or cookie concerns,
here are the issues that proved to be of the greatest interest.

cheers,

terry s


Compaq Listens 24 x 7 at www.compaqworkinggroup.org

� 2001 Terry C. Shannon, IT Consultant and Publisher, Shannon Knows Compaq

Got a bone to pick with Compaq? Do you think Compaq's marketing is a
mares-nest? Do
you believe the availability of an affordable Alpha system would help deter
customers
from wandering off the reservation and aligning themselves with the Wintel
or Sun
crowd? Or do you just have a pet peeve, comment, or suggestion that you'd
love to give
to Compaq, if you only knew who to talk to? You are not alone!

Working in conjunction with Compaq's Customer Liason Office and Quality
Assurance
folks, the DECUS/ITUG/Encompass Joint Advocacy Working Group recently
completed
the first full round of issue submission and voting based on information
gathered at the
www.compaqworkinggroup.org Web site. Per the terms of the partnership
established
more than a year ago, the results were forwarded to Compaq management for
feedback.
The official responses, which were delivered to ITUG/ DECUS Joint European
Conference attendees, are summarized below.

Compaq Takes on the Top Ten

During 4CQ00 and 1FQ01, the online advocacy mechanism collected some 130 raw
issues which were consolidated into 23 ballot items. Close to 500
participants cast votes
during the balloting process, yielding a Top Ten Issues list.

Not surprisingly, Marketing and Promotion and Branding and Positioning of
Enterprise
Products garnered 54 percent and 44 percent of the votes respectively.
Accordingly, these
issues were consolidated into a single category that gained Number One
ranking. Coming
in at a close second was Low Cost Alpha Systems. Over 44 percent of the
voters (SKC
included) regarded the dearth of such offerings to be a very significant
problem.

The accuracy, content, and ease of use of the Compaq Website, as well as the
quality of
the firm's Online Capability, drew an equal ~36 percent of the votes and
thus were
merged into a single third-place category. Compaq's Technical Support Center
was the
key Services-related issue, and fourth overall. Next on the list was
Pricing, particularly
with respect to the price and value of Compaq's enterprise products. Nearly
one-third of
the voters had their say on this issue.

A quarter of the voters expressed concern about Compaq's Third-party
Development
Tools, Product, and Solution Portfolio. A virtually identical number of
respondents cited
Product End of Life Issues, hence these issues came in sixth and seventh on
the list.
Following closely were votes urging Compaq to Enhance VMS Mail with the
ability to
allow attachments and common internet email standards. (As any Usenet lurker
knows,
the VMS community is passionate about getting its issues aired, and VMS
customers
took full advantage of the online advocacy mechanism).

Rounding out the list were Himalaya and Alpha Technology Integration and
Hardware
Product Reliability. Complete details on the Top Ten List, as well as Compaq
's
responses, are available at the online advocacy Web site at
www.compaqworkinggroup.org.

Space precludes a detailed discussion of Compaq's responses in this forum,
but the
advocacy Web site contains the 67-slide presentation that formed the basis
of Compaq
management feedback and commentary at the Lisbon conference.

Having Your Say

All users of Compaq enterprise computing systems are invited to participate
in the online
advocacy program. To make yourself heard, you'll have to complete an online
registration and profile form at the Working Group Web site. You'll be asked
to supply
your name, email address, country of residence, and the OSes you run. Once
this
demographic information is submitted, you'll receive a username and password
via
email. (You can choose to remain anonymous, in which case your name will not
be
revealed to Compaq, nor will it appear on any reports or documents generated
by the
Advocacy tool.) Participation is free of charge, and no Compaq salesperson
or
telemarketer will call you.

From now through July 4, 2001, you can visit the Web site to submit any and
all general
or product-specific issues that you deem to be important to Compaq. You can
choose
among seven predefined issue categories or create a new category; in both
cases you have
the opportunity to enter free-form comments.

At the conclusion of the issue submission phase, the Working Group will
analyze and
aggregate the submissions to produce a ballot of key issues. From August 6
through
September 21, you can return to the Web site and cast your vote for up to
five issues that
