Jordi Guillaumes i Pons <jguilla-at-attglobal-dot-net@nospam.please> wrote:
> I'm asking:

> - What is the minimum specification to run openVMS under alpha? What 
> processor? Recomendable minimun speed? Memory?

I've found that my personal minimum for RAM is 96MB without DECwindows
installed, and 112MB with it installed.  According to the SPD, the minimum
is 64MB, and starting with OpenVMS V7.2 I believe that's a requirement for
loading the OS.

> - Can I use "standard" (whatever that means) PC hardware in an alpha 
> machine? I mean, if I buy a diskless alpha, or a mobo + CPU combo... 
> will I be able to hook in any SCSI disk? What level of SCSI disk? Wide? 
> Ultra? What about graphics? What about monitors (if the box had an 
> original graphics card).

I'd *really* recommend against this, it doesn't sound like you're ready to
try and build a system (shoot, I'm not).

However, you should be able to use 3rd party SCSI HD's and you can use most
3rd party SCSI CD-ROMs if they support 512-byte blocks.  Get a DEC/Compaq
graphics card.  As for monitors, a good MultiSync monitor should work.  They
type of SCSI disk narrow, wide, UW, etc. will depend on the system you get.

> - I've seen some "clonic" alpha boxes, usually running linux. Can I load 
> VMS on those boxes?

Maybe, in some cases it's possible.  However, for your first system you
really should go with a supported configuration.

> - I'm running VAX VMS under the hobbyst license, and AFAIK there is an 
> equivalent program for the alphas. What about OSF/1, Tru64 or whatever 
> the name of the former DEC unix? Any chance of getting ultrix? (just to 
> taste it :)).

The Hobbyist program also covers Alpha's.  The Tru64 Hobbyist program is a
separate beast.  Ultrix didn't run on the Alpha, and there is no easy way to
get it period.

Now for some varied advice.  First, before buying a machine, find out what
additional RAM for it will cost.  In some cases the cost of RAM is
prohibitive, in those cases you'll only want to get the machine if it has
more than enough RAM for what you want to do.  

While a 24-Bit graphics card is nice, it can be cheaper/better to get a
100Mbit ethernet card, and display your desktop on a PC.

You can get DEC 3000 class machines fairly cheap, but they're TurboChannel,
you're probably better off going for an AlphaStation with PCI slots.  BTW, a
PWS 433au or better is a *REALLY* nice system :^)  

Do a lot of research.  Figure out what you want, what the price is, look at
your options for getting one.  BTW, you might want to check
http://www.islandco.com, I've bought parts for my PWS 433au from them,
they've got good prices and are hobbyist friendly.

