Kenn Humborg <kenn@excalibur.research.wombat.ie> writes:
> This DECserver is second-hand, and didn't come with any software.
> A websearch found a downloadable WWENG1.SYS, but I get the impression
> that WWENG2.SYS is a more feature-rich image that you have to pay
> for.

  WWENG1 was the original DECserver 700 code. WWENG2 is "DECserver Network
Access Software" (DNAS). WWENG1 was just a quick hack to get the hardware
out there, while WWENG2 was a slower hack 8-( [It has 2 different parsers
and consists of at least 3 different outsourced pieces of code as well as
the old WWENG1 code.]

  AFAIK, DECservers which factory-default to WWENG1 are licensed to run
WWENG1, and ones which factory-default to WWENG2 are licensed to run that
code or WWENG1. The license comes from having the server hardware. There
were also two other ways you could upgrade your license from WWENG1 to
WWENG2:

  1) Buying an upgrade kit which included an additional RAM SIMM and the
     license.

  2) Having a DS700 on support or warranty and reporting a bug against
     WWENG1 which was fixed in WWENG2 - the CSC would give you the WWENG2
     image and permission to deploy it.

  You can apparently buy a license/media/doc kit from Compaq - part number
DT-A5003-NV, $644, "DNAS ALL PLATFORM LIC/MEDIA KIT". This is the usual
rip-off pricing for this - many vendors give away their terminal server
software updates, and even Cisco - not known for their low prices - only
wants $300 (list, discounted to $243) for combined hardware/software sup-
port on the 2511, a 16-port terminal server that includes a high-speed
sync port and full routing capabilities.

  You can also get it from Digital Networks (KT-DNAS0-00) for an unknown
price, since they don't seem to publish a price list.

