In article <e0bcd60f.0106050341.31fac5@posting.google.com>, florent.carli@insa-lyon.fr (Mr Flo) writes:
>Well what is a DECserver ? I basically know what a hub/switch/router is ...
>but I just can't figure the DECserver out... though I tried ...
>"Multiprotocol terminal/host connections over Ethernet LANs"
>"The DECserver 90TL provides multiprotocol terminal/host connections over Ethernet LANs."
>well that does not tell me much in fact ... then what is a "terminal server" ?

A terminal(/printer) server (AKA communication server) offers connections
from/to serial-lines and (some of them) to parallel-lines (Centronics or
Dataproducts) over LAN (means Ethernet) via some protocols (LAT, TELNET,
Appletalk, IPX) to hosts (or other terminal servers).

btw. the "TL" in 90TL means "TCPIP and LAT". Earlier Servers have been
"LAT only" (like the 90L and 90L+ about 10 years ago and the DECserver
100 and DECserver 200 about 15 years ago)

So you can use it to
1) offer terminals a (bidirectional) connection to hosts
2) offer modems a (bidirectional) connection to hosts
3) offer hosts a (bidirectional) connection to a (serial/parallel) printer
4) offer hosts a (bidirectional) connection to a modem (pool)
5) offer hosts a (bidirectional) connection to devices with serial consoles
	(PABX, Routers, Servers, UPS, ...)
5) offer (bidirectional) serial line connections over LAN (SL-TS-LAN-TS-SL)
and so on

The term "terminal server" is used for this kind of equipment for more than
15 years. M$'s variant of NT5 (called Terminal Server Edition since months now)
has nothing to do with this technology !!

Is this enough ?

