HP has a method of reducing the size of groups by sending out a notice that
"you have N weeks to find a job in another group inside HP or you're
terminated".  This is nicer than just a pink slip, and tends to retain
skilled workers.  Does the Q do the same thing?  (It seems unlikely, given their 
general managerial ineptitude.)  Anyway, if they do, then somebody may have
decided to take the exit door and on the way out left the Q this ad as a
parting gift. 

Which isn't to say that incompetence didn't play a factor - something's
clearly wrong when any part of your company is giving the competition free
advertising.  And somebody who remained with the company should have caught
this.

Speaking of firing the incompetent, here is a classic example. (Obtained
second hand, from a programmer who worked there at the time, but was not
involved in the incident.) 

PacBell once put a bit of code into production which was supposed to not
charge for telephone calls lasting fewer than 5 seconds.  The idea being
that these were wrong numbers and shouldn't be charged.  It worked like a
charm but unfortunately the code did not check the minutes and hours
fields, and it took the company more days than they had backup tapes to
figure out that their income had suddently dropped to 55/60 of what it had
been, and to trace down the cause.  As a consequence the good people of
California were graced with several million dollars of free telephone
service.  Once PacBell figured out the problem heads rolled like it was the
French revolution, with everybody even remotely responsible being shown the
door - including managers many levels above the programmer. 

