In article <e0bcd60f.0106050813.3da117c9@posting.google.com>, florent.carli@insa-lyon.fr (Mr Flo) writes:
>can a DECserver be introduced into a conventionnal cisco network ?
>(with hubs, switches and cisco routers ?) how ? (why is there BNC
>plugs on them ?)

Of course. Even a CISCO router (with IOS) is able to act as a terminal server
(with TELNET, and if Multiprotocol/Enterprise Edition then also with LAT,
Appletalk and IPX) for its asynchronous serial lines (CON, AUX). It is likely
no standard use of a CISCO router, but it is working since about a decade now.

Why BNC ? That is another question. The DECmumble 90 equipment is/was
designed to insert in the DEChub90 (and later also in the DEChub900).
At the time this equipment was designed, Thinwire Ethernet (which is
also the electrical core of the DEChub90 backplane) was far more in use
than UTP. The DECserver 90M (the successor of the DECserver 90TL) has/had
UTP in addition (and more RAM, plus Flash)

>I have a dec network with hubs, routers, and DECserver (and an alpha
>station) all connected to a kind of chassis (I think that is the word).
>it's pretty old/slow now, and I want to migrate it to a network with
>usual hubs, switches and routers ... but keeping the access to the
>alpha station and to the printers connected to the DECserver ...
>I can't figure out the whole DEC/VMS thing, I'm still trying to
>compare with what I call a "normal" network
>(by the way I'm wondering if what I'm saying makes any sense ...)

Ah, the old confusion of what a hub is ;-)
In DEC terminology, a hub is a backplane, where you insert modules
like repeaters, bridges/switches, [b]routers and/or servers.

Some others (like you) use the word 'hub' as a synonym for repeater.
And a few others use the word 'hub' as a synonym for switch (while
a switch may be store&forward, cut-through or if optimal both).

I understand it as you have a DEChub90, which is a backplane with a
Thinwire bus (and some tokenring pins) and a management bus and have a
DECserver (and probably DECrepeater 90C or DECrepeater 90T) inserted.
And you want to migrate to a more modern = switched network.

But the word 'usual' is strange. Why is CISCO more usual than any other
network supplier (like DEC/DNPG, Cabletron/Enterasys, Nortel/Bay, ...).
DEC invented Ethernet and is not 'unusual' in networks ;-)

Your problem is only the DECserver with its BNC ? Use a mediachanger
or keep the DEChub90 with its DECrepeaters just for this purpose (and
connect all other ethernet connections to your new/external/'usual' switch).

But check also at http://www.dnpg.com for the DECswitch90.
You can introduce switching to your old DEChub90 backpanel as well.

