The last time I had to erase a TK50 cartridge with a degausser
was more than 7 years ago: however, I did use a hand-held
AC line powered bulk eraser (I think mine was made by
Nortronics).  Although this unit is strong enough to erase
VHS and S-VHS tapes, it was not srong enough to erase a TK50
while it was still in it's cartridge: hence my suggestion that
the leader be pulled out.  I haven't yet found a permanent
magnet that is strong enough to do the job: but there are some
very strong permanent magnets on the market, so there could
be one.  For this particular application, you don't have to
completely erase the tape: you just have to scramble the
formatting information at the beginning enough that the drive
won't recognize it, and will re-write it.

Magnetic fields decrease in strength very rapidly with distance,
particularly from small permanent magnets.  The old stories
from the 1960s of how you could wreak havoc on a big corporation
by running down the aisles of their tape library with a big
magnet in your hand are just stories: it won't work with any
magnet small enough for you to carry, especially with the magenetic
media we have now.  In order to get the very high data densities
we have now, the magnetic media requires a very strong field
to change a domain.  Digital media is even more resistant to changes
than analog media due to it's "square loop" properties, so an
eraser that will work on audio and video tapes may not completely
erase a digital tape.  I've even seen AC magnets that were strong
enough to affect old 'ferrite' tapes that were not strong enough
to really erase 'chrome' or type 3 audio cassettes: there is actually
that much difference in the properties of the magnetic media.

