kaliushkin@att.net (Dan Kalish) wrote:

> Hi, all!
> 
> I've had a lot of experience with IBM mainframes and IBM PS/2
> microcomputers, as a programmer, and concerning the latter, hardware, 
> installation of operating systems, LAN, etc.
> 
> However, I've had almost no experience with DEC products (although I did
> some work with a PDP-1 30 years ago) and would like to learn VMS at
> home.
> 
> How do I go about doing it?  Apparently,
> 
> I could get a PDP-11 but would have to build a neighborhood power
> generator and probably reinforce the floor.  :-)  

> I could pick up a
> VAXstation on eBay: currently they've got 
>  Digital VAXstation 4000/60 Unix Workstation
>  DEC 3100-M38 SYSTEM
>  DEC VAX STATION 3100 with Floppy.  (What are the differences between
> these three Vaxen?).  

The VAXstation 4000/60 is the fastest of these, and it nicer in various
other ways.  It would run VMS, maybe (unlikely) ultrix.  Ultrix was DEC's
unix before the current one, OSF-1/Digital Unix/Tru64 unix.  Ultrix no
longer has any support from DEC, and I don't think legal licenses are
available.  The current flavor of unix never ran on vaxes, only alphas.

The other two vaxes are probably very similar, maybe the same.  The 3100
family of vaxes have a firmware restriction that keeps them from working
with a system disk larger than a certain size I can't remember.  But the
size isn't very big by today's standards.  These would also run VMS.

A VAXstation 4000/90 would be much nicer than any of these others.  On the
outside it looks just like the 4000/60.  These are fairly common.  There
are a few vaxstations that are even faster, but they seem less common.

Even better would be one of the older alphastations.  These run VMS (most
models) Digital Unix (most models) or sometimes Windows NT (no longer
supported).  The DEC 3000 series systems are often available at ebay and
elsewhere, as are some of the older PCI-based systems.  Virtually all of
the alpha workstations will be faster than the VAX workstations.

You should run the best OS available for DEC systems, which means VMS. 
Look at www.openvms.compaq.com.  There's a link to the OpenVMS FAQ, which
you will want to get and read.  There is also a link to the VMS
documentation set, available on-line.

VMS is available for hobbyist use for free.  (See the FAQ.)  You can
borrow CD to install from, or you can buy the hobbyist CD for not much
money.  (See the FAQ.)



 
> Once I get such a computer, where do I find VMS?  In fact how do I set
> up a VAXstation?  How do I add disk drives, tape backup, floppy drives,
> and CD-ROM drives?  How about ethernet cards?  Does VMS support 
> peer-to-peer networking?

VMS supports DECnet, a very full-featured protocol used mainly between VMS
machines (and other DEC systems).  Recent versions include tcp/ip, with
several implementations available.

Most any non-ancient VMS system has built-in ethernet.  Many of the
workstations have floppies and/or CDs built in, though floppies aren't
used much.  Various SCSI tape drives work with recent VMS.  Used DLT
drives in the 15 GB range can be found at reasonable cost.

Except for SCSI disks, tapes, and CDs, you'll need to stick to components
supported by VMS.  Third-party SCSI devices most often work with VMS,
though CDs can be a bit weird.  A random third-party graphics card almost
certainly wouldn't work.

 
> Any recommendations?

First, read the VMS FAQ.

Buy an alpha workstation, not a VAX, unless your budget is severely
constrained.  If you do buy a VAX, don't buy a Q-bus machine.  They aren't
nice for beginners.
Read comp.os.vms

-- 
Robert Deininger
rdeininger@mindspring.com


