> I now understand that I'm talking about three different products: DEC
> PDPs, VAXen, and Alphas.  At what point in the evolution of these products
> did Compaq take over?  In fact, who makes the alpha CPU?  Not Intel.

DEC sold the PDP-11 line to Mentec some time before Compaq came along, I
think.  Compaq bought DEC (or was it Digital by then?), and got the VAX
and Alpha lines.  VAXes were orderable new until sometime last year,
though there were few models available and they were built several years
earlier.  Alpha systems are still actively developed, manufactured, and
sold by Compaq. 

> Now, what time frame are we talking about with "older" alphastations?
> Does "older" mean "less expensive"?  

Yes, older is generally cheaper.  The first generations used mainly the
Alpha 21064 CPU chip.  Those were made up till 1995 or so.  The next
generation used the 21164 CPU, and for the last 2 or 3 years there have
been system with various incarnation of 21264 CPUs.

The 21064 systems tend to be quite a lot cheaper than the 21164 one on the
used market.  Some of the 21164 systems are starting to become affordable
for the typical hobbyist.  There aren't many used 21264 systems yet; they
are sometimes offered as "refurbished" or almost new, and would be
appropriate for a small company with too little budget to buy brand-new
Compaq systems.

> Which ones run NT and which don't?

I'm not well tuned to the NT systems.  Some of the 21064 and 21164 systems
with PCI (and maybe ISA) buses.  I think none of the 21264 systems.

> BTW, speed isn't that important to me; my main computer is an IBM PS/2 77,
> an Intel 486 DX66 overdriven to 100 MHz.  That's fine for me.  I don't need
> 300 MHz.  Which ones would support ethernet?

I don't know any alphas without ethernet capability, but there might be a few.

> Does this mean a 3000 alpha would be good?

I tend to favor them, partly because I don't have much money.  They were
the high-performance systems of their era (roughly 1992 to 1995).  The
various early PCI and ISA systems can be somewhat less powerful, since
corners were cut to save money.

The DEC 3000 systems all used the 21064 or the slightly souped-up 21064A
and the DEC-designed turbochannel bus which predated PCI.  Clock speeds
varied between 100 and 275 MHz (with rumors of a 300 MHz system).  They
were pretty popular, there are lots on the used market, and parts are very
interchangeable between the DEC 3000 systems.  Also, they were very
robustly constructed and might well keep running for a few more decades.

There is at least one downside with these systems:  if the firmware gets
destroyed, it is probably not practical to repair and the system becomes
worthless.  The newer systems tend to have a "fail-safe" mechanism where
they can boot from a floppy to re-burn the firmware if necessary.  The DEC
3000s don't have that.  I've never personally seen a system that expired
this way, however.

There is a collection of DEC 3000 information at this URL, describing the
various systems:

http://www.phys.ufl.edu/~prescott/linux/alpha/dec3000-sysinfo.html

All of these systems have built-in SCSI and ethernet, and typically 8-bit
color graphics.

> > Most any non-ancient VMS system has built-in ethernet.  Many of the
