It's unlikely to have *all* the documentation. It probably
has more than enough to keep the VS4000-90 and VAX 7000
humming along nicely, but it will probably miss out anything
from much before 1990 (if you are doing self-maintenance
you need to think about peripherals too).

> from the titles if they are what's needed to maintain these machines.
> I'm currently trying to buy it to see if it's what I need, but Compaq
> is having trouble making it available.

Really? It is (or was) a 3 CD set and would set a customer
back something like $5000 per annum ... I would have
thought they'd have couriered one round
straight away wrapped in an invoice :-)

I *believe* that this CD set is produced quarterly
and pressed by an external vendor. I ordered a similar
CD set some years ago and the deal was that they
would place orders with the pressing plant
every quarter and the plant would then deliver something
like six to nine weeks after that. Throw in a few
extra weeks for it to be posted from COMPAQ to you.
If you are unlucky (liek I was) you could wait 
5-6 months (by which time my machine was back in
action ...), if you are lucky it could be as little as
3 months.

> Somewhere someone must know what documentation DEC provided their
> self-maintenance customers � I need to speak to that person.

Before CDs, we used to order whatever sounded nearest
to the Technical Manual for whichever machine we cared
about. Then we would scour that manual for docs that looked
like they might be useful.

BTW: you are very unlikely to be able to find anything
to allow you to do component level repair on either of
those machines (even assuming you have the necessary
*expensive* re-working stations to cope with the high
density IC packages). Board-level repair (i.e. board 
swapping and cable pulling) is certainly possible.

Look for system maintenance manuals and illustrated
parts breakdowns (IPBs).


Antonio

