If you have the correct ECU for WIndows NT, then just run it from the ARC
Console. It's a choice in the menu system.
Once it is running, then exit with a save. The removal of the PCI card from
the config should have happened automagically.

Actually, if it was a PCI card that had been removed then it should be out
of the configuration already, that is the nature of the beast. The ECU is
for the internal builtin stuff on the system and the EISA bus.

We built a number of models of the 2100.

We built two large pedastal versions, the first had 8 EISA slots and 3 PCI
slots, the next generation had 8 PCI slots and 3 EISA slots. We also build
some rackmount style units that were full height and half height.

This is one the first mainstream Alpha's that we designed and built here in
North America, I successfully installed Windows NT on the first units off
the pre-production line and demoed them to Corel who promptly purchased them
to use as servers.

The most unique feature about these machines is the use of an airplane wing
on the memory module, to promote colling on th ememory controllers.

