interlocks, and pulling out a couple of feet of tape (very carefully)
past the leader.  You can then use the bulk eraser right up in contact
with the tape to erase the first foot or so.  You will still need
a very strong eraser, a little battery powered unit or a tape head
demagnatizer or a CRT degausser won't work.

If you happen to have access to a strong enough bulk eraser, there
may be units that will erase the tape while it's still in it's
cartridge.  I personally have never encountered such a unit.
The media used in DDT tapes really requires quite a strong magnetic
field to change.

>
>3.  How were the tapes originally written or formatted or initialized?

Couldn't even guess based on the information available.

