  So you are making the transition from network hardware to network 
  software, and host protocols, and to host operating systems...

:If anybody has a cool web page explaining the basics, ...

  Basic OpenVMS tutorials exist.  Start with the OpenVMS FAQ and with 
  the tutorial and introductory materials referenced by the OpenVMS FAQ.

  Specifically for networking topics, you may well end up with a more 
  generic protocol discussions (hardware and software) since these 
  networking questions are not particularly specific to any platform.

:"what is thinwire?", 

  The IEEE 802.mumble standards for Ethernet-style networking provide 
  different transmission media, with "fat" wire coax being the original 
  typical medium (10Base500, IIRC) (well, "original" if you don't want to
  include and discuss stuff like AlohaNet, but I digress :-), ThinWire 
  (10BaseT, IIRC) being a thinner coax with BNC connectors, and the 
  twisted-pair RJ45-style wiring (10BaseTP and 100BaseTP, IIRC) stuff 
  being rather common in recent times.  (This is hardware, not software.)

:"LAT and IP ?", etc ...

  The one gets asked regularly in the Ask The Wizard area -- the
  two are largely equivilent, though LAT is optimized for on-LAN
  activity and cannot be routed, while telnet is somewhat more 
  involved and thus can be routed (since the underlying IP can 
  be routed).


